import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR-0159: Optimizer domains dissolve into ext tools, on a trigger","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0159-opt-domains-dissolve-into-ext-tools.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0159-opt-domains-dissolve-into-ext-tools.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0159-opt-domains-dissolve-into-ext-tools.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0159-optimizer-domains-dissolve-into-ext-tools-on-a-trigger" tabindex="-1">ADR-0159: Optimizer domains dissolve into <code>ext</code> tools, on a trigger <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0159-optimizer-domains-dissolve-into-ext-tools-on-a-trigger" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0159: Optimizer domains dissolve into ext tools, on a trigger”">​</a></h1><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to “Status”">​</a></h2><p>Proposed — 2026-10-27. Owners: <code>opt-graph</code> (was <code>graphopt</code>), <code>opt-kernel</code> (was <code>kernelopt</code>).</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The platform&#39;s shape is: an <strong>engine</strong> implements an operation, an <strong>ext</strong> ships it as a command (<code>libhnl_ext_*.so</code> + manifest), and <code>hnl</code> is a dumb host that discovers and dispatches. That is why <code>hnl</code> contains no tool code.</p><p>The two optimizer domains do not follow it. <code>graphopt</code> and <code>kernelopt</code> are Python + torch projects with their own drivers, CLIs and bench harnesses, invoked directly by their author. Nothing outside them can call &quot;optimize this model&quot; without knowing their internals.</p><p>Two facts pulled in opposite directions:</p><ul><li><strong>The endpoint is obvious.</strong> An optimizer is a command. The product surface is <code>hnl optimize --domain graph</code> / <code>--domain kernel</code>, with the domain logic behind an ext and the research core behind a port.</li><li><strong>The code is not ready.</strong> It changes hourly; the referee is being built; and it is Python against a Rust host, so dissolving now means paying an FFI seam on a surface that has not stopped moving.</li></ul><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Adopt the endpoint, and gate it behind explicit triggers rather than a date.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Domains stay labs for now.</strong> <code>opt-graph</code> and <code>opt-kernel</code> keep their own CLIs, drivers and harnesses. No ext wrapper yet.</li><li><strong>Dissolve when all three triggers hold:</strong><ul><li>a <strong>caller that is not the researcher</strong> (CI job, product, another domain) needs the capability;</li><li>the domain&#39;s public surface has been <strong>unchanged for four weeks</strong>;</li><li>the <strong>referee is shared</strong> (<code>bench/benchkit</code> usable by any domain), so a tool and a lab measure the same way.</li></ul></li><li><strong>When triggered, wrap — do not rewrite.</strong> The Python core is exposed through a port/adapter (<code>lib-triton</code>, <code>lib-*</code> family), and the ext is a thin command that calls it. Rewriting the search in Rust is not part of this decision.</li><li><strong>Naming:</strong> an <code>opt-*</code> family (<code>opt-graph</code>, <code>opt-kernel</code>). A third member (e.g. <code>opt-schedule</code>) is created only on the rule of three — if the schedule space stays a module inside <code>opt-kernel</code>, it is not a domain.</li><li><strong>The generic core stays a library.</strong> <code>egraph</code>, <code>discovery</code>, <code>eqsat</code> are domain-free; domains are rules + cost model + adapters. A new domain reuses the core rather than forking it.</li></ol><h2 id="visibility-adr-0150" tabindex="-1">Visibility (ADR-0150) <a class="header-anchor" href="#visibility-adr-0150" aria-label="Permalink to “Visibility (ADR-0150)”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0150 puts the tooling tier — <code>ext-*</code>, <code>host-cli</code>, <code>host-lsp</code>, <code>kit-*</code>, the remaining <code>lib-*</code> ports — <strong>private until done</strong>. Dissolving a domain into an <code>ext</code> therefore moves it into that tier. The consequence, stated so it is not discovered later:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>artefact</th><th>tier</th><th>visible</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>lib-kernel-ir</code> (ops, grades, costs, denotations)</td><td>vocabulary</td><td><strong>public</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>compile-triton</code> (Term → Triton)</td><td>tooling (like <code>compile-js</code>, <code>compile-llvm</code>)</td><td><strong>private</strong> until done</td></tr><tr><td><code>lib-triton</code> (runtime binding)</td><td>tooling</td><td><strong>private</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>ext-opt-*</code> (the command)</td><td>tooling</td><td><strong>private</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>opt-graph</code>, <code>opt-kernel</code></td><td>labs</td><td><strong>private</strong> (today: <code>graphopt</code> public, <code>kernelopt</code> private)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>So the public artefact of this whole line is the <strong>vocabulary and its denotations</strong> — the thing that lets anyone state &quot;this kernel means that computation&quot; — while the delivery stays private until it is done. That is consistent with the platform&#39;s own split: public substrate, private tooling.</p><ul><li><strong>Positive:</strong> productization becomes packaging, not research; the freeze protects the research until the surface is stable; a second consumer forces the API to be honest.</li><li><strong>Negative:</strong> the FFI seam is real and will cost work at the trigger; until then, two invocation styles coexist (lab drivers vs <code>hnl</code>).</li><li><strong>Neutral:</strong> this ADR does not change the M1 plan (ADR-0160, the kernel IR, <code>compile-triton</code>, the equality gate, the search driver). It fixes <em>when and how</em> the domains become tools, not what they compute.</li></ul>`,16)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};